# Online Store — environment configuration.
# Copy to `.env` and replace the placeholder values. `.env` is gitignored.
#   cp .env.example .env
#
# ── Where to get the weftd / tenant credentials ──
# Register at the official Weft console: https://weft-kit.dev
# Once your tenant is provisioned, the console shows:
#   • WEFTD_BASE     — the weftd control-plane URL (shown on the API Keys page)
#   • WEFT_API_KEY   — a tenant API key (mint it under API Keys)
#   • WEFT_TENANT_ID — your tenant ID
#
# This app is the agentic upgrade target: a traditional REST API wired to
# weftd provisioning + a browser chat panel. The weftd/LLM creds below are
# required for skill provisioning.
#
# The OPENAI_* vars configure the remote weftd control plane's LLM endpoint.
# The local shop server only reads OPENAI_MODEL (written into the skill
# definition at provision time); the rest seed weftd's LLM configuration.
# All vars are kept here so the file can serve as a single config source for
# both the local app and your weftd deployment.

# ── OpenAI-compatible LLM endpoint (remote weftd) ──
# OPENAI_BASE_URL points at the OpenAI-compatible /v1 endpoint weftd should use.
# OPENAI_MODELS is the comma-separated model catalog weftd should register.
# OPENAI_MODEL is the default model used when a run omits `model` (also written
# into the skill definition by the local provisioner).
OPENAI_API_KEY=replace_with_openai_compatible_api_key
OPENAI_BASE_URL=https://your-openai-compatible-endpoint.example.com/v1
OPENAI_MODELS=your-model-name
OPENAI_MODEL=your-model-name

# ── Remote weftd ──
# WEFTD_BASE: URL of the remote weftd control plane (shown on the API Keys page
#   of the Weft console at https://weft-kit.dev). Replace the placeholder below
#   with the value from the console.
# WEFT_API_KEY: tenant API key from the Weft console (https://weft-kit.dev).
# WEFT_TENANT_ID: tenant ID that owns the API key.
WEFTD_BASE=https://your-weftd-endpoint.example.com
WEFT_API_KEY=replace_with_tenant_api_key
WEFT_TENANT_ID=replace_with_tenant_id

# ── Shop server (optional) ──
# SHOP_PORT defaults to 19745 if omitted.
SHOP_PORT=19745

# ── CORS (production: REQUIRED, dev: optional) ──
# The backend reflects the request Origin against this allowlist instead of
# sending `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` (which is inert for credentialed
# cross-origin fetches and misleads integrators). Unset = dev-only permissive
# reflection (the Vite dev server hitting the backend on a different port).
# For any non-localhost deployment, set this to a comma-separated list of exact
# origins:
#   DEMO_CORS_ORIGIN=https://store.example.com,https://www.example.com
# The allowlist is evaluated PER REQUEST (not at module import time), so setting
# it here takes effect even though run.mjs loads .env after the ESM imports
# resolve. Verify in production that a credentialed cross-origin request from an
# un-allowlisted origin is rejected (the CORS regression guard).
# DEMO_CORS_ORIGIN=

# ── Reverse proxy trust (production behind nginx: set to 1) ──
# The SSE per-IP connection cap + publicBaseURL only trust X-Forwarded-For /
# X-Forwarded-Host when DEMO_TRUSTED_PROXY=1 (default off → uses the socket
# remote address / Host header, which a client cannot spoof). Set this ONLY
# behind a reverse proxy you control that OVERWRITES these headers (your
# nginx/edge). Leaving it off when directly exposed prevents XFF-spoofing of the
# per-IP SSE cap (the SSE-cap regression guard).
# DEMO_TRUSTED_PROXY=
